  "content": {
    "id": "p26-q7-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "Four friends shared a box of apples. Vera took 4 more than Lynn, and 13 more than Susan. Lynn took twice as many as Diana. Susan took 6 more than Diana. How many apples did each girl take?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Vera: 34 apples; Lynn: 30 apples; Susan: 21 apples; Diana: 15 apples.\n\nLet Diana's share be $x$. Equating the two expressions for Vera's share gives $2x+4=x+19$.",
      "worked": "Let Diana take $x$ apples. Lynn takes $2x$, Susan takes $x+6$, and Vera takes both $2x+4$ and $(x+6)+13$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}2x+4&=(x+6)+13\\\\2x+4&=x+19\\\\x+4&=19\\\\x&=15\\end{align*}$$\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Lynn}&=2(15)=30\\\\\\text{Susan}&=15+6=21\\\\\\text{Vera}&=30+4=34\\end{align*}$$\n\nTherefore, Vera took 34 apples, Lynn took 30 apples, Susan took 21 apples and Diana took 15 apples. The remaining relationship also holds: $34-21=13$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 92
  },
